+# Cancellation
+
+For whoever changes cancellation eligibility or helps a member who cannot collect a pantry order. Members can
+cancel in the app only before packing starts; after that, they call the pantry for a volunteer's decision. Late
+cancellations left packed fresh food on the shelf, even after a banner asked members to cancel earlier.
+
+## Cutoff
+
+Cancellation closes two hours before the pickup slot starts, when the packing shift begins. The cutoff itself is
+excluded: for a 17:00 pickup, a cancellation at 14:59 is allowed; at 15:00 or later it is refused.
+[`can_cancel`](../../larder/cancellation.py) checks eligibility using the pickup start and the current time.
+
+## Why packing sets the cutoff
+
+By October 6, volunteers had found packed bags left on the shelf for three Saturdays in a row. That day there were
+nine, containing milk, bread and greens; members were cancelling at 16:50 for a 17:00 pickup, after packing. A banner
+asking members to cancel earlier had been in place since summer, yet 25 bags of fresh food had already been thrown
+away that October.
+
+The packing shift starts two hours before each slot. Cancellations before packing cost the pantry nothing;
+cancellations after it waste a packed bag. The cutoff therefore follows the start of packing. These reports and
+the agreed behavior come from [issue 31](../../notes/issue-31.md).
+
+## After the cutoff
+
+A member who cannot come after the cutoff calls the pantry, where volunteers decide what to do. The agreed refusal
+message includes the pantry's phone number so the member can make that call.
+
+## Known limits
+
+The repository implements the eligibility check only; it contains neither a refusal message nor the pantry's phone
+number. A caller presenting a refusal must supply that message and number to support the agreed phone fallback.
